<BaseLayout title="Where Los Angeles Runs Out of Land — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Short afternoons on the Los Angeles coast, 2022–2026: Carbon Beach and the Santa Monica Pier, the South Bay from Manhattan Beach to Redondo, the bluffs of Palos Verdes and the glass Wayfarers Chapel, and the Queen Mary and the canals of Long Beach.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2026</p>
  <h1>Where Los Angeles Runs Out of Land</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Short afternoons on the coast, from Malibu to Long Beach.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>Short outings · February 2022 – February 2026</strong><br />
    Carbon Beach in Malibu → the Santa Monica Pier and the bluffs above it → Venice → Hermosa, Manhattan and Redondo
    Beach → the bluffs of Palos Verdes Estates → Wayfarers Chapel → Long Beach harbor and the Naples canals<br />
    One Route 66 sign · one glass chapel · two views of the Queen Mary · a lot of sunsets ·
    and Wangcai on the last one
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-la-coast.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-la-coast.webp'} alt="Map of the Los Angeles coast from Malibu to Long Beach, with photo stops at Carbon Beach, the Santa Monica bluffs and pier, Venice, Manhattan Beach, Hermosa Beach, Redondo Beach, Palos Verdes Estates, Wayfarers Chapel, Long Beach harbor and Naples." /></a>
    <figcaption>The places in this post. Each one is placed where its photos were taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Most of the trips on this site are long drives. This post is the opposite: short afternoons on the coast of Los
    Angeles, usually an hour or two, often timed for the sunset. None of them had enough photos for a post of its own,
    so they are collected here, roughly from north to south.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other posts here, everything below is reconstructed from the camera roll: what was photographed,
    where, and at what minute. The 2022 and 2023 photos are mine; from 2024 on many are my wife's, from afternoons we
    spent together.
  </p>

  <h2 id="malibu-santa-monica">Malibu and Santa Monica</h2>
  <p>
    The earliest one is from <strong>Carbon Beach</strong> in Malibu, on February 26, 2022. The beach sits below a
    long row of houses, and the way down is a narrow public walkway between two of them. At 1:22 pm the tide was coming in
    over flat wet sand, with the hills of Malibu running off to the west.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'carbon-beach.webp'} alt="Foamy waves washing over flat wet sand on a long beach, with houses and green hills along the shore under a sky of streaky clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Carbon Beach, Malibu, 1:22 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In Santa Monica, one January evening in 2023, at 6:18 pm, the pier
    was a strip of lights out on the dark water, seen from the bluff above it, with the big parking lot on the sand below.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'palisades-pier-night.webp'} alt="The Santa Monica Pier lit up at night, seen from above, with a large parking lot full of cars and lamps in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pier from the bluff, January 6, 2023, 6:18 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A year later, on January 23, 2024, we walked out onto the <strong>Santa Monica Pier</strong> in the afternoon.
    The Ferris wheel and the yellow roller coaster of Pacific Park stood at the far end against a clear blue sky (the park has its own section in
    <a href="/misc/amusement-parks">the amusement parks post</a>). A few minutes later a gull was standing on the
    railing.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sm-pier-wheel.webp'} alt="The Santa Monica Pier with its Ferris wheel and yellow roller coaster, seen across the water from the pier, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pacific Park from the pier, 4:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sm-gull.webp'} alt="A white and gray gull with a yellow beak standing on a metal railing above blue water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A gull on the railing, 4:14 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Near the entrance to the pier stands a sign: a shield with "Santa Monica 66" and, below it,
    "End of the Trail", for the end of Route 66.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sm-route66.webp'} alt="A white Route 66 shield sign reading Santa Monica and End of the Trail, against a blue sky, above a red and white food stand." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The end of Route 66, 4:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A little further south is <strong>Venice</strong>. On January 5, 2024, we walked along the boardwalk in the late
    afternoon and ended up at the skatepark on the sand. At 4:25 pm the sun was low over the ocean behind the
    palms, and a skater was climbing out of one of the bowls.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'venice-skatepark.webp'} alt="The curving concrete bowls of the Venice skatepark with a skater at the edge, palm trees and a low sun behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Venice skatepark, 4:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="south-bay">The South Bay</h2>
  <p>
    South of the airport the coast becomes the South Bay: Manhattan Beach, Hermosa Beach and Redondo Beach, one after
    another, each with its own pier. In 2022 I went to two of them. On March 27, with friends, it was the
    <strong>Redondo Beach Esplanade</strong>, the road along the top of the bluff. At 5:04 pm the sun was laying a
    bright path across the water, and a little later we walked out on a rock jetty.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'redondo-2022-sun.webp'} alt="Sunlight reflecting in a bright path on the ocean below a bank of clouds, with an empty beach and a fence in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Redondo Beach, March 27, 2022, 5:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'redondo-2022-esplanade.webp'} alt="A view down a dry bluff to tiled roofs and a long beach, with the ocean and a line of clouds beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view from the Esplanade, 5:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On June 6, 2022, it was <strong>Hermosa Beach</strong> and its Pier Plaza. On the plaza, a bronze surfer crouches on his board on a round pedestal, facing the sand.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hermosa-surfer.webp'} alt="A bronze statue of a surfer crouching on a surfboard on a round dark pedestal, with the beach and the Hermosa Beach pier behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The surfer statue at Pier Plaza, 2:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    <strong>Manhattan Beach</strong> is in the photos twice. The first time was on March 9, 2024, at sunset. From the end
    of the pier at 5:48 pm, two ships were waiting offshore, with a line of hills on the horizon behind them. Six minutes
    later, looking back toward land, the pier ran straight in to the town, with people lined along its railings.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mb-ships.webp'} alt="Two ships on the horizon of a rippled sea under a golden sky, with a line of hills behind them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ships offshore, from the pier, 5:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mb-pier-town.webp'} alt="The Manhattan Beach pier running from the water to the town, with people along its railing, the beach, and houses on the hill in evening light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking back at the town, 5:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The second time was almost two years later, on February 21, 2026, and this time Wangcai came along. At 5:43 pm
    the sun was setting at the bottom of one of the streets that run down the hill to the beach. Ten minutes later the
    sun was gone, and the clouds over the water had turned pink and orange.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mb-street-sunset.webp'} alt="The sun setting into the ocean in an orange sky, seen down a street between houses, with a palm frond in the corner." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset down the street, 5:43 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mb-clouds.webp'} alt="Pink and orange streaks of cloud in a golden evening sky over the ocean, with a low headland on the horizon and a dark beach below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>After the sun went down, 5:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By 6:04 we were at the foot of the pier, and Wangcai sat for her photo by the railing, in her pink leash, with the
    houses on the hill behind her.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'mb-wangcai.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog sitting on a concrete walkway with her mouth open, a pink leash, and a pale green railing behind her." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai at the Manhattan Beach pier, 6:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="palos-verdes">Palos Verdes</h2>
  <p>
    South of Redondo the beaches end and the coast rises into the Palos Verdes Peninsula. On June 23, 2024, we stopped
    on the bluffs along Paseo del Mar in <strong>Palos Verdes Estates</strong> just before sunset. Along the coast, the
    curve of the bay ran back toward the city under small white clouds. Looking west, at 7:35 pm, the sun was
    behind a band of clouds, sending rays up across the sky and a path of light down onto the water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'paseo-bluff.webp'} alt="A grassy bluff top above the ocean, looking along the curve of a bay to a distant city and mountains under small white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bay from the bluffs, 7:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'paseo-sunset.webp'} alt="Sun rays fanning out from behind dark clouds over a calm ocean, with a golden path of light on the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sunset from Paseo del Mar, 7:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3 id="wayfarers">Wayfarers Chapel</h3>
  <p>
    Earlier that year, on January 14, 2024, we spent the early afternoon at King Harbor in Redondo Beach and then
    drove down the peninsula to <strong>Wayfarers Chapel</strong>, the
    glass chapel designed by Lloyd Wright in Rancho Palos Verdes. The walls and roof are glass set in a frame of wood
    and stone, so from inside the pews you look straight out into the trees.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wayfarers-inside.webp'} alt="Inside a chapel with wooden pews on both sides of an aisle, under a peaked glass roof in a wooden frame, with trees visible through the glass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Inside the chapel, 4:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wayfarers-glass.webp'} alt="The outside of a glass chapel with a pointed wooden frame among tall trees, with ferns and a stone wall in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The chapel from outside, 4:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Outside, the chapel stands among tall trees, next to a stone tower and a low stone building with bird-of-paradise
    flowers in front.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wayfarers-tower.webp'} alt="A glass chapel with a wooden frame beside a tall stone tower and a low stone building, with orange bird-of-paradise flowers and potted plants on a brick path." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The stone tower and the chapel, 4:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The chapel stood on the edge of a slow landslide. The next month, in February 2024, it closed because the ground
    under it was moving, and later in 2024 it was taken apart.
  </p>

  <h2 id="long-beach">Long Beach</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Long Beach</strong> is at the other end of the bay, past the ports. On January 8, 2023, at 4:49 pm, the
    marina by Shoreline Village was full of sailboats under a sky turning pink, with the downtown towers behind the
    masts. Across the water lay the <strong>Queen Mary</strong>, the old ocean liner moored in Long Beach. At 5:02 her three red funnels stood out against the evening clouds.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lb-marina.webp'} alt="A marina full of sailboats and motorboats reflected in still water, with downtown towers and a pink and blue evening sky behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The marina by Shoreline Village, 4:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lb-queen-mary-dusk.webp'} alt="The Queen Mary ocean liner with three red funnels, seen across calm water under gray evening clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Queen Mary at dusk, 5:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two months later, on March 11, 2023, I saw the Queen Mary again, this time from the water, on a short boat ride
    around the harbor on a gray afternoon. At 1:37 pm the boat passed a Carnival cruise ship, and three
    minutes later the Queen Mary herself.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lb-cruise-ship.webp'} alt="A large white cruise ship with a red winged funnel on gray water under an overcast sky, seen from a small boat." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A cruise ship in the harbor, 1:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lb-queen-mary.webp'} alt="The Queen Mary with her three red funnels and black hull, seen from the water under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Queen Mary from the water, 1:40 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last Long Beach photo is from <strong>Naples</strong>, the neighborhood of canals on the east side of the city. On August 16, 2025, at 4:21 pm, a canal ran straight between rows of houses with boats tied up at
    their docks.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'naples-canal.webp'} alt="A straight canal lined with houses, palm trees and small boats tied at docks, under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A canal in Naples, 4:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <!-- FIRE-WEEKEND START: self-contained block (heading + text + figures). Delete from here to FIRE-WEEKEND END to remove it. -->
  <h2 id="january-2025">January 10–11, 2025</h2>
  <p>
    On January 10, 2025, three days after the Palisades and Eaton fires broke out, we drove west to Santa Monica in the
    afternoon. At 2:49 pm, from the freeway, the sun was an orange disc in a sky of brown smoke.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fire-freeway-sun.webp'} alt="An orange sun in a sky full of brown smoke, above dark trees beside a freeway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sun through the smoke, 2:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From the bluffs above the beach at 3:08, a band of smoke lay over the bay and the pier. By 3:21, a white plume
    from the Palisades Fire was rising from the mountains up the coast. Later that afternoon we went on to the Baldwin
    Hills, which are in <a href="/misc/views-from-above#baldwin-hills">the views from above post</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fire-smoke-pier.webp'} alt="A wide band of brownish smoke across the sky above a beach, with the Santa Monica Pier small in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Smoke over the bay, 3:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fire-plume.webp'} alt="A white plume of smoke rising from mountains along the coast, above a beach lined with palm trees and white buildings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The plume up the coast, 3:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The next day we were in Redondo Beach. At 4:24 pm the boats in King Harbor sat on calm water under a clear sky, and
    by 4:56, on the beach, the sun was going down into a dark band of haze over the water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'smoke-king-harbor.webp'} alt="Sailboats moored by a small palm-lined dock in a calm harbor under a clear evening sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>King Harbor, January 11, 4:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'smoke-sunset-jetty.webp'} alt="A bright sun low over the ocean in a band of orange haze, above a rock jetty and a dark beach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset by the jetty, 4:56 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    At 5:04 the last of the sun sank under a red band of haze.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'smoke-sunset.webp'} alt="The last of the sun on the horizon under a thick red-orange band of haze, with waves breaking on the beach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The last of the sun, 5:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <!-- FIRE-WEEKEND END -->

  <p>
    That is most of what the camera roll holds of the Los Angeles coast: short afternoons, a lot of sunsets, and a
    few places, like the glass chapel, that are no longer there to visit.
  </p>
